  "body": " Periodontal Disease and Heart Attack Risks   Medical researchers have uncovered strong statistical links between oral health and cardiovascular conditions: of people who have suffered a heart attack had periodontal disease (an inflammation of the gums), while only of heart-healthy people have this disease.  Suppose that in a certain community, heart attacks occur with a baseline probability of . Let represent the event that an individual has a heart attack, and let represent the event that an individual has periodontal gum disease.  Match each probability description or algebraic notation on the left with its correct calculated numerical value or expression on the right. Note that there are extra numbers on the right that act as distractors!                         First identify your baseline priors: and its complement . Then locate the two conditional values from the text. Use the Law of Total Probability to compute the total rate of gum disease before evaluating your final Bayes' Theorem formula.    "
