  "title": "Uniform Random Variable",
  "body": " Uniform Random Variable   By completing the problems provided below, you will gain practice with:                      Interactive Exercises    Written Exercises   The Discrete Uniform Random Variable   Through this comprehensive exercise, you will explore the properties of the discrete uniform random variable, analyze an intuitive dice-rolling scenario, and ultimately derive its general theoretical formulas.       Part A: Suppose you roll a single fair 6-sided die. Let the random variable represent the exact number that is rolled. Find the probability distribution function for .         Part B: For the single 6-sided die roll described in Part A, compute the expected value and the variance .         Part C: The discrete uniform random variable is used to model experiments where there are a finite number, , of equally likely outcomes. Based on this definition, explain why the random variable from Part A is uniform.         Part D: Provide another real-world scenario where the variable of interest would be a discrete uniform random variable. Fully describe it here: make sure to explicitly define your random variable, state its probability distribution, and explain why your random variable is uniform.         Part E: Write a general formula that can be used for the probability distribution function of any discrete uniform random variable. Assume the random variable takes on values 1 through . Explain the reasoning behind your answer.         Part F: Use your generalized formula from Part E to analytically derive the theoretical formulas for the expected value and variance of a general uniform random variable.    Review your foundational algebraic summation shortcuts for common integer series:         Advanced Discrete Variables: Block Game Spawn Gaps   Recall the block-matching game, Tetris, that utilizes a 7-Bag Random Generator . The engine places exactly one copy of each of the seven unique shapes ( ) into a bag, shuffles them completely, dispenses all seven, and then immediately repeats the process with a fresh bag.  A player tracks the game queue across two consecutive bags (Bag 1 followed by Bag 2). Let the discrete random variable represent the spawn distance gap between the first bag's -piece and the second bag's -piece.  We define this distance by counting the steps from one piece to the next. For example, if the first -piece is the last card in Bag 1 (position 7) and the next -piece is the first card in Bag 2 (position 8), the distance is .    Let be the position of the -piece in Bag 1 (ranging from 1 to 7) and be its relative position inside Bag 2 (ranging from 1 to 7). The absolute index position in Bag 2 is . Therefore, the distance random variable can be written as: Remember that because each bag is shuffled independently, and are independent discrete uniform random variables on the integer interval .      Determine the finite support space (the set of all possible integer values) for the random variable . What are the minimum and maximum possible spawn distances between two consecutive -pieces?       Independent discrete uniform random variables on the integer interval . Is also uniform? Explain your answer.        Calculate the probability of experiencing the absolute minimum distance gap ( ) and the absolute maximum distance gap ( ).       How far apart would you expect the I pieces to be? In other words, what is the expected spawn gap?        Calculate the probability of experiencing a spawn distance gap of exactly seven pieces ( ).       "
