  "number": "5.6.3",
  "title": "Poisson Processes: Aerospace Structural Integrity Audits.",
  "body": " Poisson Processes: Aerospace Structural Integrity Audits   An aerospace manufacturing quality control audit shows that microscopic surface material flaws in high-performance carbon-fiber fuselage panels occur completely randomly and independently at a constant average rate of one flaw every 270 square feet. A technician pulls a newly manufactured rectangular section measuring exactly 9 feet by 13 feet for testing.  Let denote the total number of structural flaws contained within this specific panel segment. Because flaws are distributed randomly across a continuous spatial region, follows a Poisson distribution.      Determine the appropriate expected rate parameter (the mean number of flaws) for this specific 9-foot by 13-foot fuselage panel.           First, calculate the total surface area ( ) of the inspected fuselage panel by multiplying its physical length and width dimensions: Next, scale the baseline spatial material flaw density rate ( flaws per square foot) by the target panel area to find the segment's expected value parameter :           Using your rate parameter from Part A, calculate the probability that this specific aerospace panel contains absolutely no flaws.           To find the probability of observing exactly zero events in a continuous spatial Poisson process, evaluate the Poisson probability mass function (PMF) at using your unrounded rate parameter :  Rounded to four decimal places, the final probability that the high-performance structural panel contains no surface flaws is approximately 0.6483 (or a 64.83% chance).       "
