  "title": "Analyzing Cryptographic Hashing Probabilities.",
  "body": " Analyzing Cryptographic Hashing Probabilities   Bitcoin mining utilizes an algorithmic process called cryptographic hashing, which structurally behaves like a sequence of millions of independent Bernoulli trials. Each time a computing node performs a single hash, it has a tiny probability of of successfully solving the current network difficulty puzzle. Solving a block rewards the miner with a subsidy of Bitcoins.  These operations are executed by specialized Application-Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC) computers called miners. A typical enterprise miner performs approximately hashes per second. Suppose a commercial operation deploys a farm array of identical miners and runs them continuously for exactly one year.      Determine the total cumulative number of hashes performed by the 10-miner farm array over the course of a full 365-day year.        Let represent the exact number of blocks successfully mined by the farm array during the year. Explain why can be modeled as a Binomial random variable, and define its structural distribution parameters.        Explain why the Binomial model defined in Part B can be well approximated by a Poisson distribution, and calculate the appropriate value for the rate parameter .        Using your Poisson approximation model, determine the mathematical probability that the hardware cluster successfully mines at least one block over the course of the year ( ).        Find the probability that the mining operation secures between 2 and 4 blocks inclusive over the annual tracking window. In other words, compute .     "
