  "title": "Uniform Random Variable",
  "body": " Uniform Random Variable   By completing the problems provided below, you will gain practice with:                      Interactive Exercises   Continuous Distributions: Statistics Quiz Completion Times   The amount of time it takes a MATH 350 student to complete a probability exam can be modeled by a continuous uniform distribution between 25 and 55 minutes. If one students is selected completely at random from the class, compute the following probabilities for that student.      Find the probability that the student requires more than 51 minutes to complete the exam ( ).      Incorrect. This is the probability that the student takes less than 51 minutes ( ). Remember to find the area of the upper tail corresponding to \"more than.\"      Correct! For a uniform distribution on the interval , the total width is minutes. The target width for taking more than 51 minutes spans from 51 to 55:      Incorrect. It looks like you divided the target width of 4 minutes by the upper boundary of 55 instead of the actual total width of the distribution support interval ( minutes).     Incorrect. This distractor assumes a baseline width calculation slip where the interval was mistakenly calculated out of 25 instead of 30. Check your subtraction.     Incorrect. This matches only a 2-minute interval width over the 30-minute support space. Review the limits bounding the upper tail area.         Find the probability that the student completes the exam in a time frame between 31 and 35 minutes ( ).       Correct! The length of the target sub-interval is minutes. Dividing this by the total distribution support width of 30 minutes gives:   Notice that because the uniform distribution has a constant flat probability density, any sub-interval of width 4 within the main domain boundaries has an identical likelihood weight.     Incorrect. This looks like a mistake where a width of 5 minutes was used in the numerator ( ) instead of evaluating the true net difference between 35 and 31.     Incorrect. It looks like you divided the sub-interval width of 4 by the upper bound of 35 instead of using the total uniform interval width parameter ( ).     Incorrect. This matches a full 10-minute interval block over the support range. Re-verify your boundary subtraction steps.     Incorrect. This matches a 3-minute sub-interval step. The distance between 31 minutes and 35 minutes is exactly 4 minutes.        Continuous Distributions: Greeley Winter High Temperatures   Historical climate records indicate that the daily daytime high temperature during the month of December in Greeley, Colorado follows a continuous uniform distribution over the interval from 32 to 52 degrees Fahrenheit. A business traveler arrives in the city on a randomly selected day in December.      What is the probability that the daytime high temperature will be above 43 degrees Fahrenheit ( )?      Incorrect. This is the probability that the high temperature is below 43 degrees ( ). Remember to isolate the upper portion of the interval for \"above.\"     Correct! The total width of the uniform distribution support is degrees. The target interval width for high temperatures above 43 degrees spans from 43 to 52:      Incorrect. It looks like you divided the target width of 9 by the upper bound of 52 instead of using the total uniform interval width parameter ( ).     Incorrect. Do not confuse the boundary threshold value (43) with a probability ratio. Calculate the width of the target zone over the total zone width.     Incorrect. Review your boundary subtraction steps. The distance between 43 and 52 is exactly 9 units.        What is the probability that the daily high temperature will be between 34 and 46 degrees Fahrenheit ( )?      Incorrect. This matches a target width of 8 units instead of the true distance between 34 and 46.     Incorrect. This is the cumulative probability area to the left of the upper boundary ( ). You still need to subtract the lower tail area under 34 degrees.     Correct! The length of our target sub-interval is degrees. Dividing this by the total distribution support width of 20 degrees gives:      Incorrect. This is the lower tail probability of experiencing a high temperature cooler than 34 degrees ( ).     Incorrect. Make sure to divide the sub-interval distance by the true width parameter ( ) rather than an upper boundary score.        Find the expected daily high temperature and its standard deviation.  Expected =   Standard Deviation =         Correct! For any continuous uniform random variable on the interval , the expected value maps to the exact spatial midpoint:          Correct! Evaluate the continuous distribution variance parameter using its analytical shortcut identity: Take the square root of the variance to determine the standard deviation:           Written Exercises   Analytical Derivations of Mean and Variance   Let be a continuous uniform random variable defined over the real-numbered interval , where . The constant probability density function (PDF) is given by: and elsewhere. Complete the analytical derivations below to verify its expected value and variance.       Part A: Prove that the expected value (mean) for a continuous uniform random variable on the interval is:          Part B: Show that the mathematical variance for a continuous uniform random variable on the interval is given by the formula:        "
