  "title": "Exponential Random Variable",
  "body": " Exponential Random Variable   By completing the problems provided below, you will gain practice with:                      Interactive Exercises   Systems Engineering: Tech Support Ticket Arrival   The idle time (in minutes) separating consecutive inbound service tickets at an IT help desk is modeled by a continuous exponential distribution with a mean of minutes.      What is the probability that the idle time between consecutive incoming service tickets is at least 15 seconds?  Probability =                Find the probability that the idle time passing between consecutive incoming service tickets is between ten and fifteen seconds.  Probability =                Suppose that exactly 2.0 minutes have passed since the last support ticket arrived, and the system is currently completely idle. What is the conditional probability that the dashboard will have to wait at least an additional 15 seconds before the next ticket hits the firewall?   =         Correct! Also, notice that this is the same answer as in part A. The exponential distributions possess the memoryless property , written formally as .  Knowing that the system has already waited for minutes has absolutely no bearing on future waiting times. The clock effectively \"resets,\" making the probability of waiting an additional minutes (15 seconds) identical to the specified conditional probability:          Reliability Engineering: Industrial Machine Repair Timelines   The continuous time (measured in hours) required for an automation engineer to repair a critical assembly line machine is modeled by an exponential distribution with probability density function (PDF) given by: and elsewhere.      Identify the expected value (mean repair duration), , directly from the structure of the provided probability density function.   hours.        Correct! For any standard continuous exponential density function written in the form , the expected value is . Since , the mean is exactly 7 hours.          Compute the standard deviation ( ) for the machine repair duration random variable .   hours.        Correct! One of the unique defining structural properties of any continuous exponential distribution is that its expected value and standard deviation are mathematically identical ( ).          Calculate the probability that a randomly scheduled machine repair will require at least 12.5 hours to finish. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places   hours.        Correct! To calculate the probability that the variable exceeds a threshold, we evaluate the upper tail survival function, . Plugging in our parameters:          Calculate the probability that a randomly scheduled machine repair will require at least half an hour to finish. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places.   hours.        Correct! .          Suppose that a repair job has already been going on for exactly 12 hours and is still incomplete. What is the conditional probability that the final duration will end up taking at least 12.5 hours in total? Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places.   hours.        Correct! . OR we can use the memoryless property of the exponential distribution to simplify this computation. Here, the system has already survived for hours, and we want to know if it will survive for at least an additional hours to hit 12.5 total hours: This is the probability that we computed in part d.         Proof Derivation: Expected Value of an Exponential Distribution   Let be a continuous random variable following an exponential distribution with rate parameter , where its probability density function (PDF) is defined as for .  Arrange the blocks below in the correct logical sequence to derive the expected value. Recall: .      Set up the integral definition for the expected value:      Get ready to apply integration by parts! Assign your terms:      Substitute these terms into the integration by parts formula :      Evaluate the limits for the first term , noticing that the upper limit goes to 0 by L'Hôpital's Rule:      Compute the remaining integral. First find the anti-derivative:     Evaluate the anti-derivative from 0 to :        Written Exercises   Auditing the Apex Bank Billboard Campaign   A regional mega-bank, desperate to steal customers from its competitors, launches a flashy billboard campaign asserting they have the absolute fastest tellers in the tri-state area. Their main marketing slogan claims that \"virtually all of our customers are served in less than 10 minutes!\"   An amused data scientist opens an account, sits in the lobby with a hidden stopwatch, and collects a large sample of service times. They conclude that the customer service times are actually exponentially distributed with a long-run mean of minutes. Let us investigate what the bank's marketing department mathematically means by \"virtually all.\"      State the probability density function (PDF), , for this service model. Then use this PDF to compute the proportion of customers who are actually served in under 10 minutes.    This is the same as finding the probability that customers are served in under ten minutes!        State the cumulative distribution function (CDF), , for this service model. Use it to recompute the proportion of customers served in under 10 minutes.        While the bank's average (mean) service time is 5.5 minutes, the exponential distribution is heavily skewed toward longer wait times. Find the median service time to determine how fast the middle customer (the 50th percentile) actually gets served.       Analytical Derivations using the MGF   Let be a continuous random variable following an exponential distribution with rate parameter , defined by the probability density function (PDF) for . Complete the analytical derivations below to determine its moment generating function and theoretical variance.      Derive the equation for the moment generating function of the exponential distribution. State the constraint required for the domain of .        Differentiate your moment generating function expression from Part A to find the first and second moments about the origin ( and ). Use these to show that the theoretical variance for an exponential random variable is .       "
