  "number": "7.3.2",
  "title": "Continuous Distributions: Normal Distribution Exam Times.",
  "body": " Continuous Distributions: Normal Distribution Exam Times   We previously modeled the amount of time that it takes a MATH 350 student to complete a probability exam as a continuous uniform random variable. A more realistic and accurate model would be the normal distribution.  Assuming exam completion times follow a normal distirbution with a mean of 40 minutes and a standard deviation of 5 minutes, compute the following probabilities.      Find the probability that a randomly selected student requires more than 51 minutes to complete the exam ( ).       Incorrect. This is the probability that the student takes less than 51 minutes ( ). Remember to use the complement rule to find the area of the upper tail for \"more than.\"       Correct! First, standardize the target value to find the Z-score:   Using a standard normal table or technology, the area to the left of is . Finding the upper tail gives:        Incorrect. It looks like you might have used the variance ( ) in the denominator instead of the standard deviation ( ) when calculating your Z-score.       Incorrect. This was the answer from the uniform distribution version of this problem. Under a normal curve, the probability density drops significantly as you move out into the tails.       Incorrect. This is the probability of a value falling more than 2 standard deviations away ( ). Recheck your subtraction arithmetic for the Z-score numerator.         Find the probability that a randomly selected student completes the exam in a time frame between 31 and 35 minutes ( ).       Correct! First, convert both boundary thresholds into their respective standard normal Z-scores:   Find the cumulative area to the left for both scores from your standard normal distribution curve tables:   Subtracting the lower boundary area from the upper boundary area yields the interval probability:        Incorrect. This is only the cumulative area to the left of 35 minutes ( ). You still need to subtract the lower tail area corresponding to times under 31 minutes.       Incorrect. It looks like you added the two boundary probabilities together instead of subtracting them to find the area contained between them.       Incorrect. Remember that unlike the uniform distribution, intervals of equal width do not have equal probabilities in a normal distribution because the curve is non-linear and changes height.       Incorrect. This is the lower tail probability of a student completing the exam in fewer than 31 minutes ( ).      "
